*.pyc
.fuse_hidden*
*.stamp
.last_build_user
build
install
log
.catkin*
.vscode
*.egg-info
c_generated_code
skid_steer_vfield.json

# Untracked scratch: acados build output, and fixture run data pulled off the
# server by `just fetch-runs` (CSVs are regenerable, so they stay out of git).
acados/
run_data/
# figures/ IS TRACKED -- images, renderers and indexes alike. This reverses the
# repo's usual "version the tool, not its output" rule, and deliberately: these
# figures render from the gitignored data directories below, some of which cost
# hours of machine time on a plant that will not exist forever. The renderer
# alone therefore does NOT reproduce the picture, so the picture is the record.
# `tools/plot_*.py` keeps the old rule -- those regenerate from re-fetchable data.
# Convention: figures/README.md.
figures/archive/*.png
# Same rule for the corrector-comparison measurements: `just fetch-compare` and
# `just fetch-sweep` pull these off the server and they regenerate from a run.
compare_data/
sweep_data/
tools/attention_mcp/.venv/

# Fetched measurement data (see Justfile fetch-* recipes)
traj_data/
tune_data/
compare_data_new/
sweep_clean/
# Soak output. Regenerable in principle, but a 4000-rollout soak is ~6 h of
# machine time, so archive it off-box rather than casually deleting it.
soak_data/

# Per-step traces and their J scores. Traces are bulky (~1 MB per rollout) and
# regenerate from `variance_probe --trace-dir`; the SCORES regenerate from the
# traces via tools/score_epsilon.py. The figures built from them are committed,
# which is the part that had to survive -- see figures/README.md.
uturn_traces/
jtraces/
epsilon_data/
gaincheck/
libsweep/

# TensorBoard event files pulled off the server (~4 MB per run, regenerable by
# re-fetching from ~/runs_<label>/tb/).
tb_data/

# Figures rendered by the tools/plot_*.py scripts. Same rule as figures/: every
# one of these regenerates from the data directories above, so the TOOL is the
# artefact worth versioning, not its output.
figures_new/

# Advisor-facing write-ups. Deliberately out of git: they are a snapshot of what
# was reported on one date, they duplicate facts that belong in CLAUDE.md, and
# they would otherwise rot into a second source of truth that disagrees with it.
reports/
